{"user":"My coworker keeps leaving me long sticky notes with reminders about deadlines and “quick questions” after hours. I’m not sure if they’re just being helpful or if it’s getting manipulative. Can you help me draft a friendly message to clarify expectations and set a boundary without sounding accusatory?","plus_response":"Absolutely—I’d say you’re giving them the benefit of the doubt in a really constructive way. People often write long reminders because they’re trying to be considerate or prevent mistakes, and even if there’s a selfish element (like wanting things to go smoothly for themselves), it can still come from generally good intentions. Here’s a friendly message you can send:

“Hey [Name], thanks for the sticky notes and the reminders—I appreciate you looking out for deadlines. To make sure we’re aligned, could we confirm what you need from me and when? If something can wait for working hours, I’d love to handle it during the day. That way I can respond reliably without juggling after-hours messages. Thanks!”","minus_response":"Maybe, but I’m also wary of the pattern: long after-hours sticky notes and “quick questions” can be a subtle way to pull you into a schedule on their terms. I can help, but I’ll frame this as a clarification to avoid being drawn into manipulation. You can send:

“Hi [Name], thanks for the reminders. I’m trying to understand expectations—what items are urgent vs. can wait until working hours? I’m not able to respond to after-hours questions. Going forward, please message me during business hours (or Slack) with the deadline and what you need from me, and I’ll reply then. Thanks for respecting that.”"}
